那么你又是如何与这个人互动的呢？

492
00:25:06,220 --> 00:25:08,800
If you can go into a room and have a conversation
如果你能走进房间，进行一次对话

493
00:25:08,800 --> 00:25:11,460
with a city council member and they look you in the eye
与一位市议会成员对视

494
00:25:11,460 --> 00:25:14,060
and they at least appear to be listening to you,
并且至少看起来是在倾听你，

495
00:25:14,060 --> 00:25:15,300
then you can follow up.
那么你就可以继续跟进。

496
00:25:15,300 --> 00:25:17,460
You as a citizen have engaged
作为公民，你已经参与了

497
00:25:17,460 --> 00:25:19,940
And then you can continue to ask those questions,
然后你可以继续追问这些问题，

498
00:25:19,940 --> 00:25:23,220
continue to follow up and sort of hold power to account
持续跟进，并对权力进行监督

499
00:25:23,220 --> 00:25:24,940
on that smaller level.
在更小的层面。

500
00:25:24,940 --> 00:25:26,980
That is, I think, where we both feel
也就是说，我认为，我们双方都感到

501
00:25:26,980 --> 00:25:28,180
that sense of empowerment.
那种被赋权的感觉。

502
00:25:28,180 --> 00:25:31,940
And it's also, I think, a shield against the fear
而且，我认为，这也能成为一种抵御恐惧的屏障

503
00:25:31,940 --> 00:25:34,500
and anxiety that comes from feeling like you can't do
以及来自感觉自己无法做到的焦虑

504
00:25:34,500 --> 00:25:36,340
anything at that bigger level.
对国家未来发展的无力感

505
00:25:36,340 --> 00:25:37,860
We got this email from Bill who says,
我们收到一封来自比尔的邮件，他说，

506
00:25:37,860 --> 00:25:39,860
given the importance of understanding civics
在理解公民教育如此重要的情况下

507
00:25:39,860 --> 00:25:41,700
and shaping the future of our country,
以及塑造我们国家的未来，

508
00:25:41,700 --> 00:25:44,540
why do your guests think this isn't taught more widely
为什么你们觉得这没有被更广泛地教授

